Cross-check of the optional files: the copies at
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoupVaillant/Monocypher/4.0.2/src/optional/monocypher-ed25519.c
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoupVaillant/Monocypher/4.0.2/src/optional/monocypher-ed25519.h
are different from the copies in the tarball only in the first line
("__git__" in place of "4.0.2"). This is the same release-script
substitution as in monocypher.c. So all four files are the 4.0.2 release.

Why monocypher-ed25519 (DESIGN.md §1.2 D4, §2, A1): Kome signs with RFC 8032
Ed25519, and uses SHA-512 only inside the signature. Core monocypher's
crypto_eddsa_* functions are EdDSA with BLAKE2b-512. This is not
RFC 8032, so Kome does not bind them. Kome.Crypto binds
crypto_ed25519_key_pair, crypto_ed25519_sign and crypto_ed25519_check.
BLAKE2b, X25519, XChaCha20-Poly1305 and crypto_wipe come from
monocypher.c.

Note on strictness: crypto_ed25519_check refuses S >= L. But, by design, it
accepts non-canonical encodings of R and A (y >= p, or x = 0 with the sign
bit set). Wycheproof ed25519_test.json tcId 151 is such a signature, and
crypto_ed25519_check accepts it. So Kome.Crypto.Verify itself checks the
RFC 8032 §5.1.3 encodings of R and A before it calls crypto_ed25519_check
(see kome-crypto.ads, Is_Canonical_Point).
